[{"id":3,"slug":"a-year-and-a-half-of-letters-and-new-boots","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/paper-route/a-year-and-a-half-of-letters-and-new-boots/","date_gmt":"2026-10-13T09:00:00","title":{"rendered":"A Year and a Half of Letters, and New Boots"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>This is roughly a year and a half of Paper Route. I did not think I had fifteen letters in me. It turns out the route has plenty to say if I stop to listen.</p>\n<p>The biggest lesson was to write things down on the same day. A fact about a shop or a street fades in a week, and the feeling around it fades even faster.</p>\n<p>The new boots, for the record, are brown, and they already have a crease across the left toe. Thank you for reading, and write back if you walk a route of your own.</p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"The anniversary issue: what I got right, what I got wrong, and why the boots matter."},"author":2,"categories":[1],"tags":[],"_embedded":{"author":[{"id":2,"name":"Paper Route","slug":"paperroute"}]}},{"id":4,"slug":"the-shop-that-mends-clocks","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/paper-route/the-shop-that-mends-clocks/","date_gmt":"2026-09-08T09:00:00","title":{"rendered":"The Shop That Mends Clocks"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>On Weaver Street there is a clock mender with forty clocks in the window and no two showing the same time. He told me this is on purpose: each one is waiting for a part.</p>\n<p>He works with a loupe screwed into one eye and a radio tuned low to the cricket. When I deliver his post he holds up one finger, which means wait, and finishes his spring before he looks up.</p>\n<p>I asked how he knows the right time. He pointed at the church tower across the road and said it is wrong too, but at least it is wrong in public.</p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"A narrow shop on Weaver Street, forty clocks, and no two of them showing the same time."},"author":2,"categories":[1],"tags":[],"_embedded":{"author":[{"id":2,"name":"Paper Route","slug":"paperroute"}]}},{"id":5,"slug":"redrawing-the-route-on-the-kitchen-table","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/paper-route/redrawing-the-route-on-the-kitchen-table/","date_gmt":"2026-08-11T09:00:00","title":{"rendered":"Redrawing the Route on the Kitchen Table"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>Each August the postal service redraws the routes. Streets move from one walker to another and the whole district shuffles. I do my own redraw first, on the kitchen table with a pencil and my 1987 map.</p>\n<p>I try to make the route shorter, with fewer hills. The official version is usually the same shape as mine, give or take a street, which says either that I have learned something or that the city only allows a few good answers.</p>\n<p>This year I lose the two streets by the canal. I will miss the heron.</p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"Once a year the routes are redrawn, and I do my own version first, in pencil, on the kitchen table."},"author":2,"categories":[1],"tags":[],"_embedded":{"author":[{"id":2,"name":"Paper Route","slug":"paperroute"}]}},{"id":6,"slug":"heat-and-the-long-shadows-of-four-oclock","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/paper-route/heat-and-the-long-shadows-of-four-oclock/","date_gmt":"2026-07-14T09:00:00","title":{"rendered":"Heat and the Long Shadows of Four O’Clock"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>In July I start at five in the morning to finish before the heat. By noon the pavement on Marsh Street is hot enough to feel through my soles.</p>\n<p>There is a short stretch of the route in the shade of a viaduct, and I take my water break there every day. Pigeons have learned my schedule and arrive a minute before I do.</p>\n<p>By four in the afternoon the shadows are long and the streets start to fill again. I am usually home by then, with my feet up.</p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"July on the route means starting at five and finishing before the pavement gets too hot."},"author":2,"categories":[1],"tags":[],"_embedded":{"author":[{"id":2,"name":"Paper Route","slug":"paperroute"}]}},{"id":7,"slug":"a-spring-of-closed-roads","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/paper-route/a-spring-of-closed-roads/","date_gmt":"2026-05-12T09:00:00","title":{"rendered":"A Spring of Closed Roads"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>In one fortnight this spring the route lost three roads: one to resurfacing, one to a burst water main, and one to a street party that was announced on a lamp post two days before.</p>\n<p>The temporary signs pointed me in a loop that came back to where I started. I stood there for a moment, then walked the other way and delivered the letters in the wrong order, which turned out to be fine.</p>\n<blockquote><p>Plans on a route are suggestions. The letters will get there.</p></blockquote>\n<p>A supervisor said that to me my first week, and I wrote it on the inside of my satchel flap.</p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"Resurfacing, a burst main and a street party all landed in the same fortnight."},"author":2,"categories":[1],"tags":[],"_embedded":{"author":[{"id":2,"name":"Paper Route","slug":"paperroute"}]}},{"id":8,"slug":"the-kiosk-that-sells-only-batteries","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/paper-route/the-kiosk-that-sells-only-batteries/","date_gmt":"2026-04-07T09:00:00","title":{"rendered":"The Kiosk That Sells Only Batteries"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>By the bus station there is a kiosk the size of a wardrobe that sells batteries and nothing else. Every size, from watch cells to the big square ones for lanterns.</p>\n<p>I asked the woman who runs it why she does not sell phone chargers too. She said chargers are a different kind of customer. Batteries are for people in a hurry who have a particular thing in mind.</p>\n<p>She has run it for eleven years. She told me she has never once had a day with no customers.</p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"A tiny stall by the bus station, one product, and a surprisingly loyal clientele."},"author":2,"categories":[1],"tags":[],"_embedded":{"author":[{"id":2,"name":"Paper Route","slug":"paperroute"}]}},{"id":9,"slug":"reading-a-street-from-the-kerb","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/paper-route/reading-a-street-from-the-kerb/","date_gmt":"2026-02-10T09:00:00","title":{"rendered":"A Short Guide to Reading a Street From the Kerb"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>You can read a street from the kerb. Bins left out late mean a working week. A child’s bike on its side means a school holiday. Curtains drawn at noon mean either illness or a night shift.</p>\n<p>I do not read these signs to pry. I read them because they tell me whether to knock, to leave a card, or to leave the parcel with the neighbour who always says yes.</p>\n<p>The signs are never certain. I have been wrong about the curtains at least four times, and each time the person behind them was kind about it.</p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"Bins, bikes and curtains: what you can learn about a block before you reach the first door."},"author":2,"categories":[1],"tags":[],"_embedded":{"author":[{"id":2,"name":"Paper Route","slug":"paperroute"}]}},{"id":10,"slug":"forty-minutes-on-the-wrong-side-of-the-river","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/paper-route/forty-minutes-on-the-wrong-side-of-the-river/","date_gmt":"2026-01-13T09:00:00","title":{"rendered":"Forty Minutes on the Wrong Side of the River"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>The footbridge by the gasworks closed for repairs in January, so for three weeks I took the road bridge, which adds forty minutes and a long stretch beside a motorway slip road.</p>\n<p>On that stretch I found a tool library, a shop that mends umbrellas, and a laundrette with a shelf of paperbacks you can swap. None of it is on my usual route.</p>\n<p>I have asked the council when the footbridge reopens. They said spring. I have mixed feelings about that.</p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"A closed footbridge sent me the long way round, and the long way was not wasted."},"author":2,"categories":[1],"tags":[],"_embedded":{"author":[{"id":2,"name":"Paper Route","slug":"paperroute"}]}},{"id":11,"slug":"fog-and-the-sound-of-the-trams","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/paper-route/fog-and-the-sound-of-the-trams/","date_gmt":"2025-11-04T09:00:00","title":{"rendered":"Fog and the Sound of the Trams"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>In thick fog the route turns into sound. I can tell the Number 6 tram from the Number 14 by the pitch of the wheels on the bend by the school.</p>\n<p>The wires overhead hum a little when a tram is a block away. If the hum stops, the tram has stopped, and I step off the kerb more carefully.</p>\n<p>I would not say I enjoy fog. But I notice more in it, and by ten the world comes back to normal and I miss it slightly.</p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"On foggy mornings I navigate by ear, and the tram wires are the best map there is."},"author":2,"categories":[1],"tags":[],"_embedded":{"author":[{"id":2,"name":"Paper Route","slug":"paperroute"}]}},{"id":12,"slug":"the-bakery-with-no-sign","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/paper-route/the-bakery-with-no-sign/","date_gmt":"2025-09-09T09:00:00","title":{"rendered":"The Bakery With No Sign"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>There is a bakery on Hollis Lane with no sign, no menu in the window and no website. You find it by the smell, which reaches the corner a full minute before you reach the door.</p>\n<p>They bake two things: a seeded loaf and a plain one. By eight the seeded loaf is gone. I get mine on the way back from the sorting office, when the baker hands it over still warm and says nothing.</p>\n<p>I have been told that this is not good business. The queue says otherwise.</p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"You find it by smell, and the queue starts at ten past six."},"author":2,"categories":[1],"tags":[],"_embedded":{"author":[{"id":2,"name":"Paper Route","slug":"paperroute"}]}},{"id":13,"slug":"house-numbers-that-skip","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/paper-route/house-numbers-that-skip/","date_gmt":"2025-08-05T09:00:00","title":{"rendered":"House Numbers That Skip"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>On Pellam Road the numbers go 11, 12, 14. Nobody remembers a 13. The residents say it was never built, and the council says it was, and the one house that might be 13 has a brass 12A on the door.</p>\n<p>Three streets over, two houses are both number 22, one facing the other. The postal service solved this years ago by a rule that lives only in the heads of the people who walk there.</p>\n<p>Most of my job is knowing things that are written down nowhere. I find that odd and a little pleasing.</p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"Why number 13 is missing, why 22 comes twice, and what to do when the street disagrees with itself."},"author":2,"categories":[1],"tags":[],"_embedded":{"author":[{"id":2,"name":"Paper Route","slug":"paperroute"}]}},{"id":14,"slug":"what-a-wet-june-does-to-paper","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/paper-route/what-a-wet-june-does-to-paper/","date_gmt":"2025-06-10T09:00:00","title":{"rendered":"What a Wet June Does to Paper"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>In a wet June the envelopes in my bag swell until they will not stack. Stamps lift at the corners. Ink addresses written in a hurry bleed into a blue cloud.</p>\n<p>The old hands on the route keep a roll of plastic bags in the bottom of the satchel, and I now do the same. One bag per street, sealed at the top, opened on the doorstep.</p>\n<p>It is a small thing, but it is the difference between delivering a letter and delivering a damp rectangle.</p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"Rain makes envelopes swell, stamps lift and addresses run. Here is how we cope."},"author":2,"categories":[1],"tags":[],"_embedded":{"author":[{"id":2,"name":"Paper Route","slug":"paperroute"}]}},{"id":15,"slug":"a-hardware-shop-that-closes-for-lunch","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/paper-route/a-hardware-shop-that-closes-for-lunch/","date_gmt":"2025-05-06T09:00:00","title":{"rendered":"A Hardware Shop That Closes for Lunch"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>There is a hardware shop on Calder Street that closes from twelve to one. A hand-lettered sign says so, and the sign has not changed in the nine years I have passed it.</p>\n<p>The owner, Mr. Okafor, sells loose screws by weight from a wall of small drawers. You tell him the size and he knows the drawer without looking. I have watched him do it with his back turned.</p>\n<blockquote><p>Nobody has ever asked me to open at lunch. They just come back at one.</p></blockquote>\n<p>I think about that line when I am tempted to be available all day.</p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"Mr. Okafor shuts at noon sharp, and the whole block has arranged its day around it."},"author":2,"categories":[1],"tags":[],"_embedded":{"author":[{"id":2,"name":"Paper Route","slug":"paperroute"}]}},{"id":16,"slug":"the-map-in-my-left-pocket","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/paper-route/the-map-in-my-left-pocket/","date_gmt":"2025-03-11T09:00:00","title":{"rendered":"The Map in My Left Pocket"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>In my left pocket I carry a paper map from 1987, soft as cloth at the folds. It was my uncle’s. It still shows a lane behind the old tannery that no app has ever heard of.</p>\n<p>The map is wrong about plenty. Two streets have been renamed and one has been built over. But the mistakes are useful, because they show me where the city has changed its mind.</p>\n<p>I keep a pencil in the same pocket and mark the corrections in the margin. After a year the margin is more accurate than the map.</p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"A folded city map from 1987 still beats my phone on three streets, and I can say why."},"author":2,"categories":[1],"tags":[],"_embedded":{"author":[{"id":2,"name":"Paper Route","slug":"paperroute"}]}},{"id":17,"slug":"route-nine-in-the-first-week-of-snow","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/paper-route/route-nine-in-the-first-week-of-snow/","date_gmt":"2025-02-04T09:00:00","title":{"rendered":"Route Nine in the First Week of Snow"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>I walk Route Nine, which is forty-one streets north of the rail yard. In the first week of snow the route took an hour longer, and I learned which doorsteps are shovelled by neighbours and which are shovelled by nobody.</p>\n<p>The mailboxes tell you who is home. A cleared path to the door means someone is expecting something. A single line of footprints means a person who goes out once a day, for bread, and comes back.</p>\n<p>This is the first letter. I will write one a month or so, about the route, the maps I keep, and the shops I pass. Thanks for reading it.</p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"What changes on a delivery route when the pavements disappear under four inches of snow."},"author":2,"categories":[1],"tags":[],"_embedded":{"author":[{"id":2,"name":"Paper Route","slug":"paperroute"}]}}]